Q: Header and Footer/user/admin nav bar A: <p>The idea is good, but the implementation is not mature-enough. You need to consider many things here, listing them would be too much, firstly because I lack context of your project and secondly there are too many concerns. Therefore, instead, I'm laying out some ideas.</p>
<p>You are doing some templating to avoid code-duplication. Which is cool. But let's look at your code. It contains the entirety of <code>head</code> and in part the <code>body</code>. What if you are going to create themes? Will they all have a different head? Unlikely. So if you create multiple themse sharing the same <code>head</code>, then you duplicate code...</p>
<p>Instead, you should create a <strong>head.php</strong> template that contains your <code>head</code> and separate that concern from other templates. Your <strong>head.php</strong> should look like this:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;head&gt;
    &lt;!-- Your head tags here --&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Similarly, you could create a template that's gonna be a wrapper, you could call it <strong>body.php</strong> and it would look like this:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;body&gt;
    &lt;!-- Your structure here --&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>and a <strong>layout.php</strong> that's gonna look like this:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;?php
require_once &quot;head.php&quot;;
require_once &quot;body.php&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>Of course your <strong>body.php</strong> can require further sub-templates. You need to make sure that a template fulfills exactly one goal and, at the same time, it is fulfilling that goal entirely. Don't create multiple pages to represent different languages, instead, hydrate dynamic content based on session and/or database settings. Don't finish your template with a container div, but create a <strong>container.php</strong> that looks like this:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;div class=&quot;container&quot;&gt;
    &lt;!-- Your structure here --&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>In short, you need to think of reuse-ability, the reduction of repetition count and your structure given in the question does not properly separate structural elements. You need a template to be a structural element from start to finish of it. Hence, when you speak of <strong>container.php</strong>, for example, then it will directly translate to your container div and nothing else. So, if you are going to have 10 container divs in a page for some reason, then you <code>require</code> it ten times without being worried of a container div being already opened but the other 9 not being opened yet.</p>

Q: Serve the static files (images &amp; media) outside of my middleware in ASP.NET Core Web API A: <p><strong>Hello,</strong></p>
<p>If the images are purely static (or were generated once and then saved to disk), the requests shouldn't be processed by Kestrel at all. Routing static files through the .NET pipeline consumes resources unnecessarily—resources that web servers like nginx were specifically designed to manage efficiently by serving files at kernel speed.</p>
<p>Here is an approach, depending on your specific configuration:</p>
<p>Let nginx handle serving the files directly (0 ms .NET overhead).
Set up a dedicated block for static files in your nginx configuration that points to your &quot;wwwroot&quot; directory. When a user requests a static resource, nginx serves it directly from the disk using system calls (sendfile). Your ASP.NET Core API is not invoked, and no C# code is executed.</p>
<h3>Let nginx handle serving the files directly (0 ms .NET overhead)</h3>
<p>Set up a dedicated block in your nginx configuration for static files that points to your <code>wwwroot</code> directory. When a user requests a static resource, nginx serves it directly from the disk using system calls (<code>sendfile</code>). Your ASP.NET Core API is not invoked, and no C# code is executed.</p>
<p>Here is what the nginx configuration looks like:</p>
<pre><code>server {
    listen 80;
    server_name api.yourdomain.com;

    # Root directory pointing to your published wwwroot folder
    root /var/www/my-project/wwwroot;

    # 1. SERVE STATIC FILES DIRECTLY VIA NGINX
    # Matches common media/static extensions and bypasses .NET completely
    location ~* \.(webp|jpg|jpeg|png|gif|ico|svg|css|js)$ {
        try_files $uri =404;

        # Performance tweaks
        expires 30d;
        add_header Cache-Control &quot;public, no-transform&quot;;
        access_log off;
        sendfile on;
        tcp_nopush on;
    }

    # 2. ROUTE ALL OTHER API REQUESTS TO ASP.NET CORE
    location / {
        proxy_pass http://localhost:5000; # Kestrel address
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
        proxy_set_header Connection keep-alive;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Well, I hope I understood the question correctly 🙄</p>
<p>Have fun trying it out.</p>
